            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.456,
                0.702,
                0.543,
                0.721
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 12.6"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.736,
            0.881,
            0.794
          ],
          "content": "**Proof of (a):** In the parallelogram ABCD, AC is a transversal for AB ‖DC and also for AD ‖BC. So we get $\\Delta  ACD \\cong \\Delta  CAB$ by the ASA test. Therefore, $\\mathrm{AB} = \\mathrm{DC}$ and $\\mathrm { A D } = \\mathrm { B C }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.794,
            0.882,
            0.872
          ],
          "content": "**Proof of (b):** BC ‖AD with AB a transversal. Therefore $\\angle \\mathrm{A} + \\angle \\mathrm{B} = 180^{\\circ}$ as they are interior angles on the same side of this transversal. By similar reasoning, any two adjacent angles add up to $1 8 0 ^ { \\circ }$ . By using algebra, $\\angle \\mathrm{A} = \\angle \\mathrm{C}$ and $\\angle \\mathrm{B} = \\angle \\mathrm{D}$"
        }
      ]
    }
